MATH(3)                        P32W Unix                         MATH(3)

NAME
       math - floor, sqrt, exp, log, pow, sin, cos and the others

SYNOPSIS
       #include <math.h>

       double floor(double x);   ceil trunc round rint nearbyint
       double fabs(double x);    sqrt cbrt exp exp2 expm1 log log2
                                 log10 log1p logb sin cos tan asin
                                 acos atan sinh cosh tanh asinh
                                 acosh atanh erf erfc tgamma lgamma
       double fmod(double x, double y);  remainder pow atan2 hypot
                                 copysign fmin fmax fdim nextafter
       double frexp(double x, int *e);   double ldexp(double x, int n);
       double modf(double x, double *ip);  double scalbn(double, int);
       int ilogb(double x);   long lround(double x);
       long lrint(double x);
       isnan(x) isinf(x) isfinite(x) isnormal(x) signbit(x)
       fpclassify(x)  HUGE_VAL INFINITY NAN M_PI M_E ...

DESCRIPTION
       C's maths library, on softfp(3) (P32W has no FPU). The exact
       ones give the IEEE result bit for bit: fabs floor ceil trunc
       round (halves away from zero) rint and nearbyint (halves to
       even) fmod remainder modf frexp ldexp scalbn ilogb logb sqrt
       copysign fmin fmax fdim nextafter. Most of the others are
       worked out in 62 bits and rounded once: exp exp2 expm1 log
       log2 log10 log1p sin cos tan asin acos atan atan2 sinh cosh
       tanh asinh acosh atanh cbrt. Checked against exact values,
       each gives the correctly rounded result for 99.5% or more of
       its arguments, and otherwise the double next to it (at most
       0.51 units in the last place). pow is correctly rounded for
       whole powers up to 2047, and otherwise works its logarithm
       and y log x in 96 bits: correctly rounded too, in every one
       of 3,000 checked. hypot is within an ulp; erf, erfc, tgamma
       and lgamma, musl's, within a few. sin, cos and tan reduce
       large arguments exactly (Payne-Hanek).

       Special values follow C99 (Annex F): signed zeros,
       infinities, NaN. float and double are one type here, so the
       f and l forms (sinf, sqrtl, ...) are the same functions.

RETURN VALUE
       The result; NaN outside a function's domain.

EXAMPLES
       double r = sqrt(x * x + y * y);
       printf("%.6f\n", atan2(y, x) * 180 / M_PI);

SEE ALSO
       softfp(3), strtod(3), printf(3), cc(1)

BUGS
       errno is not set and there are no floating-point exception
       flags (math_errhandling is 0). A double operation is a
       library call, so this is slow: sin takes about 29,000
       instructions, exp 39,000, atan 18,000, pow 77,000. The
       functions keep work areas in static memory, so they are not
       reentrant (a signal handler should not use them while the
       program does).
